<h2>A near-tied top market</h2>
<p>Calendar 2024 data shows the UAE and Bangladesh essentially tied for India's largest turmeric market — UAE at USD 50.9 million against Bangladesh's USD 50.0 million, both well ahead of the third-place United States (USD 23.5 million). Total Indian turmeric exports that year reached USD 328 million across roughly 177,738 MT, and a separate trade-press estimate puts India's world market share at around 66%, making it the dominant global origin.</p>

<h2>Curcumin content: the parameter that actually drives price</h2>
<p>Turmeric's active compound, curcumin, is both the quality marker and the primary pricing lever. Standard culinary-grade turmeric runs at one curcumin level; buyers supplying pharmaceutical or nutraceutical manufacturing typically need higher, more tightly specified curcumin content and will pay accordingly. Tell us your end use, and we'll quote against the appropriate curcumin target rather than a generic grade.</p>

<h2>Sourcing regions and what differs between them</h2>
<p>We source from Salem and Erode in Tamil Nadu and Nizamabad in Telangana — three of India's most established turmeric-growing belts, each with somewhat different curcumin profiles and colour characteristics reflecting local soil and cultivar differences. If you have a preference or a specific past-supplier profile you're trying to match, tell us and we'll recommend the closest regional match.</p>

<h2>Form: finger, split or powder</h2>
<p>Whole finger and split turmeric retain quality longest in storage and suit buyers with their own grinding capability. Powder is convenient for direct use but has a materially shorter shelf life once ground, since grinding exposes more surface area to oxidation. Match the form to your processing setup and turnover speed rather than defaulting to powder for convenience alone.</p>

<h2>Import requirements in the UAE</h2>
<p>The UAE's Ministry of Climate Change and Environment (MOCCAE) handles agricultural import requirements, with emirate-level municipality food-control departments also involved in registering packaged food products. See our full <a href="/markets/uae/">UAE market guide</a> for ports, transit times and the broader documentation picture.</p>

<h2>Bulk vs. private-label packaging</h2>
<p>UAE buyers span both ends of the packaging spectrum — bulk importers supplying local repackaging and food-service operations, and retail-focused buyers wanting finished, branded packaging ready for supermarket shelves. Bulk typically moves in 25kg or 50kg bags at lower per-kilo cost; retail packaging (100g-1kg pouches or jars with your artwork) costs more per unit but captures more margin downstream if you have retail distribution. Tell us which model fits your business and we'll quote packaging options accordingly — see our <a href="/private-label-bulk-supply/">private label and bulk supply</a> page for how that process works.</p>
